:: Help Prototype Workbench [nobr] {"position":"300,4750"}
<p>At the <b>Company</b> office you can get the board out and build on <<= $company.prototype.name>> by hand. Every session is <b>one job</b> and takes <b><<= setup.workbench.actions>> actions</b>. The jobs come from a queue in random order, so you might get two hardware jobs in a row and then a test. The bar at the top shows the job you're on and what's up next.</p>
<dl class="help-stats">
	<dt>Hardware</dt><dd>Wiring leads onto the right controller pins, or soldering joints with just the right amount of heat.</dd>
	<dt>Software</dt><dd>Finding and fixing bugs in the firmware, or remapping bad memory sectors while the firmware is flashed.</dd>
	<dt>Testing</dt><dd>Setting switches so logic boards pass their test, or keeping the temperature in range during a stress test.</dd>
</dl>
<p>The job explains itself before it starts, and its clock only runs once you press <b>Start</b>. Mistakes cost time and lower the score. You can leave a job as it is at any time, but whatever is unfinished counts as failed. When the job is over, the progress is added and you're back in the office.</p>
<p>The score decides the progress: up to <b>+<<= setup.workbench.maxGain>></b> per session, plus <b>+<<= setup.workbench.flawlessBonus>></b> for a job at <<= Math.round(setup.workbench.flawlessAt * 100)>>% or better. Progress never goes past the current <a href="#" data-help="Help Prototype Progress">limit</a>, and the workbench stays closed while the prototype is at it.</p>
<p>The further along the prototype is, the more intricate the work gets: more leads and joints, narrower heat windows, more bugs, bigger logic boards and less warning, from <b><<= setup.workbench.tierAt[0]>></b> and again from <b><<= setup.workbench.tierAt[1]>></b>.</p>
<div class="help-note">Backing out before you press Start costs nothing, and the job stays at the front of the queue.</div>
